{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts sit ~+3 to +8 EUR above realized levels, especially after regime shifts; apply a downward level correction (larger when recent trend turns down).",
    "Missed mean reversion after local highs: when history shows a peak/spike (e.g., ~72–73) followed by weakening, the model keeps projecting elevated prices; enforce stronger mean reversion toward the 10–20d average.",
    "Downtrend lag: once the market starts a sustained sell-off, the model reacts too slowly and predicts sideways/up; add a negative momentum term when last 5–10d return is negative and accelerating.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: overprediction grows with horizon (outer 2–4 weeks worst); increase the downward adjustment with forecast step (e.g., small cut days 1–5, larger cut days 10–30).",
    "Overshoot persistence: model assumes high-price persistence (sticky levels) while realized path breaks lower quickly; cap forecast persistence by limiting day-to-day increases when recent volatility is high and direction is down."
  ]
}
